  "title": "Blacklisted users can bypass restrictions and unstake funds via `unstakeThroughComposer` if already in cooldown",
  "severity": "high",
  "validity": "valid",
  "quality": "",
  "user_handle": "ameng",
  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n**Root Cause:**\nThe protocol implements a `FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE` (blacklist) intended to freeze the assets and activities of malicious users. This restriction is enforced in `_withdraw` and `_beforeTokenTransfer` within the base `StakediTry` contract.\n\nHowever, the `unstakeThroughComposer` function in `StakediTryCrosschain` bypasses these standard withdrawal checks.\n1.  When a user enters the cooldown period, their assets are moved from the `StakediTry` vault to the `iTrySilo`.\n2.  The `unstakeThroughComposer` function interacts directly with the silo and the `cooldowns` mapping to withdraw funds.\n3.  Crucially, this function **does not verify** if the `receiver` (the original user) has the `FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE`.\n\n**Impact:**\nA user who has been blacklisted (e.g., for malicious activity) AFTER entering the cooldown period can still successfully withdraw their funds. By triggering a cross-chain unstake request (or if the Composer processes a pending request), the funds will be withdrawn from the silo and bridged back to the user.\n\nThis completely negates the effectiveness of the blacklist for any funds that are already in the cooldown state. Since the cooldown period is typically when an admin would identify and blacklist a malicious actor, this loophole renders the \"freeze\" capability ineffective during the most critical window.\n\n**Note:** The same vulnerability likely exists in the direct `unstake` function in `StakediTryV2` (StakediTryCooldown), as it also lacks a blacklist check on the receiver.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nAdd a check in `unstakeThroughComposer` (and `unstake`) to ensure the receiver is not blacklisted.\n\n```diff\n    function unstakeThroughComposer(address receiver)\n        external\n        onlyRole(COMPOSER_ROLE)\n        nonReentrant\n    {\n+       if (hasRole(FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE, receiver)) revert CommonErrors.OperationNotAllowed();\n        // ... existing logic ...\n    }\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "> **Note on Methodology:**\n> I initially attempted to build a PoC using the full `StakediTryCrosschain` contract. However, due to the contract's extensive inheritance tree (combining LayerZero, ERC4626, and AccessControl), the test suite consistently failed with `Yul Exception: Stack too deep`, even with `via_ir` enabled and the optimizer configured.\n>\n> To validly demonstrate the vulnerability without being blocked by compiler limitations, I created a **Logic Replication PoC**.\n>\n> This PoC uses a simplified harness (`MockStakediTryCrosschainLogic`) that replicates the **exact logic** of the vulnerable `unstakeThroughComposer` function from the source code. It isolates the specific logic flow to prove that the function allows withdrawals without checking the `FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE` on the receiver.\n>\n> **Steps to Run:**\n> 1. Create a file `test/PoC_BlacklistUnstake_Logic.t.sol` with the code below.\n> 2. Run: `forge test --match-contract PoC_BlacklistUnstake_Logic`\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity 0.8.20;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {MockERC20} from \"./mocks/MockERC20.sol\";\n\n// Simplified AccessControl\ncontract MockAccessControl {\n    mapping(bytes32 => mapping(address => bool)) private _roles;\n    bytes32 public constant FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE = keccak256(\"FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE\");\n    bytes32 public constant COMPOSER_ROLE = keccak256(\"COMPOSER_ROLE\");\n\n    function grantRole(bytes32 role, address account) public {\n        _roles[role][account] = true;\n    }\n\n    function hasRole(bytes32 role, address account) public view returns (bool) {\n        return _roles[role][account];\n    }\n\n    modifier onlyRole(bytes32 role) {\n        require(_roles[role][msg.sender], \"AccessControl: account is missing role\");\n        _;\n    }\n}\n\n// Mock Logic Contract replicating StakediTryCrosschain's unstakeThroughComposer\ncontract MockStakediTryCrosschainLogic is MockAccessControl {\n    MockERC20 public asset;\n    \n    struct Cooldown {\n        uint256 cooldownEnd;\n        uint256 underlyingAmount;\n    }\n    mapping(address => Cooldown) public cooldowns;\n    \n    constructor(address _asset) {\n        asset = MockERC20(_asset);\n    }\n    \n    // Simulate depositing and cooldown\n    function depositAndCooldown(address user, uint256 amount) public {\n        cooldowns[user] = Cooldown(block.timestamp, amount);\n        asset.mint(address(this), amount); // Simulate funds moving to silo/contract\n    }\n\n    // --- REPLICATED LOGIC START ---\n    // This is the exact logic from StakediTryCrosschain.unstakeThroughComposer\n    // We verify if this logic checks for FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE\n    \n    function unstakeThroughComposer(address receiver) external onlyRole(COMPOSER_ROLE) {\n        Cooldown memory cooldown = cooldowns[receiver];\n        uint256 assets = cooldown.underlyingAmount;\n\n        // Logic checks\n        require(assets > 0, \"No assets\");\n        require(block.timestamp >= cooldown.cooldownEnd, \"Cooldown not ended\");\n\n        // State update\n        delete cooldowns[receiver];\n\n        // Transfer funds\n        // In real contract: silo.withdraw(msg.sender, assets)\n        // Here we simulate transfer to msg.sender (the composer)\n        asset.transfer(msg.sender, assets);\n    }\n    // --- REPLICATED LOGIC END ---\n}\n\ncontract PoC_BlacklistUnstake_Logic is Test {\n    MockStakediTryCrosschainLogic public staking;\n    MockERC20 public iTryToken;\n    \n    address public user = address(0x2); \n    address public composer = address(0x3);\n    \n    function setUp() public {\n        iTryToken = new MockERC20(\"iTRY\", \"iTRY\");\n        staking = new MockStakediTryCrosschainLogic(address(iTryToken));\n        \n        staking.grantRole(keccak256(\"COMPOSER_ROLE\"), composer);\n    }\n\n    function test_PoC_Logic_Bypass() public {\n        // 1. Setup: User has assets in cooldown\n        staking.depositAndCooldown(user, 100 ether);\n        \n        // 2. Blacklist User\n        staking.grantRole(keccak256(\"FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE\"), user);\n        assertTrue(staking.hasRole(keccak256(\"FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE\"), user));\n        \n        // 3. Attempt Unstake via Composer\n        vm.startPrank(composer);\n        uint256 balanceBefore = iTryToken.balanceOf(composer);\n        \n        // This call executes the replicated logic. \n        // If the logic HAD a check like:\n        // if (hasRole(FULL_RESTRICTED_STAKER_ROLE, receiver)) revert();\n        // then this would fail.\n        // Since it doesn't, it succeeds.\n        staking.unstakeThroughComposer(user);\n        \n        uint256 balanceAfter = iTryToken.balanceOf(composer);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        \n        // 4. Verify\n        assertEq(balanceAfter - balanceBefore, 100 ether);\n        (uint256 end, uint256 amount) = staking.cooldowns(user);\n        assertEq(amount, 0);\n    }\n}\n\n```",
